{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"---\n\n# RESEARCH HANDOFF — ROUND 1 CONTINUATION\n\n## Header & Run Profile (unchanged from round 0)\n\n- Ruler: Joko Widodo (\"Jokowi\"), President of Indonesia\n- Period: 2023 (single target year; second-term, last full calendar year)\n- ISO3: IDN · Ruler ID: 2636 · Ruler-Year ID: 16200\n- Run key: `2023-population-top20-gate5-v1`\n- Job key: `dossier:2023-population-top20-gate5-v1:2023:IDN:16200`\n- Researcher role: permissive, internet-enabled\n- File-write status: BOTH supplied paths still unreachable (sandbox returns `bwrap: loopback: Operation not permitted` on every `exec_command`). Complete handoff delivered in-line per contract.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Round-1 Corrections and Decisions (responding to reviewer brief)\n\n### 0.1 Disposition rule applied across all chapters\n\nThe reviewer correctly noted that several round-0 items bundled multiple distinct claims or sources, used \"researcher assessment language\" that risks steering the judge, attributed subordinate/ministerial/institutional conduct to Jokowi without evidence of direction/knowledge/tolerance, and occasionally cited retrospective/out-of-period evidence as 2023 evidence. To address this, I am restating the source-confidence profiles and adding an explicit `ruler_attribution_evidence` field for every retained item. Items that cannot establish a positive attribution link are reclassified from `final_evidence` to `context` for the ruler chapter (they remain available for the country-year baseline).\n\n### 0.2 Temporal-fit rule\n\nFor 2023 chapters, only items whose **anchor event or decision falls between 1 Jan 2023 and 31 Dec 2023** count as **target-period final evidence**. Items anchored in 2024 (election period, sea-sand-export PP 23/2023 amendment, September 2024 poverty data, Prabowo-Gibran post-registration conduct, BRICS offer decline context, etc.) are kept as **context/retrospective** with explicit `period_drift` labels. The Constitutional Court ruling 90/PUU-XXI/2023 (16 October 2023) and the November 2023 ethics ruling on Usman are kept as final evidence for 4B/7B; the 2024 election results, BTI 2026 retrospective, and the September 2024 poverty statistics are excluded from 2023 attribution.\n\n### 0.3 Item-splitting policy applied\n\nWhere a round-0 item bundled two or more materially distinct claims, the bundled item is **decomposed** into per-claim units with new IDs and the original ID is marked `superseded_by` in the manifest. Where a round-0 item attributed subordinate/ministerial conduct to Jokowi without evidence of personal direction, the item is **re-typed** to `context` (for the ruler-attribution lens) and the precise attribution distance is recorded.\n\n### 0.4 Counter-claim discipline\n\nFor each chapter I add at least one explicit **counter-claim** item where the discovery search found contrary or mitigating evidence, so the judge is not presented with a one-sided dossier. These are labelled `counter_claim` and are still retained as final evidence when they meet traceability and source-confidence floors.\n\n### 0.5 Manifest continuity\n\nThe round-0 manifest remains the base; this round **amends** it. Every disposition change, new item, superseded item, and rejected item is listed in §6 below. The manifest is the single accounting index; nothing is silently lost.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Local Baseline — Round-1 Confirmation\n\nThe 36 round-0 local-prior facts (LF001–LF036) remain unchanged. Their **disposition is confirmed**: all 36 stay as **context** (country-year structured prior) and **none** are promoted to final evidence for ruler-attributed conduct in any chapter. The disposition reasons in the package (`I001`, `I002`, `R001`) are preserved. No local dataset was re-fetched. The local `error_methodology_ids` array is empty — no local-extraction failure needs to be flagged in the round-1 manifest.\n\nThe four local `no_evidence_found` chapters (1B and 6B) are confirmed: 1B is a documented `no_discriminating_opportunity` case (no FAS nuclear-force rows for Indonesia, no nuclear/existential-risk conduct surfaced); 6B is a documented `no D6 rows` case (no HDI/health/education/income rows in the local-prior field-key list for this country-year). Round-1 web research was targeted at 6B to fill the human-development lens with narrative priors — see §3.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Round-1 Evidence-Ledger — Chapter-by-Chapter Restatement\n\nThe IDs below supersede or extend the round-0 IDs. IDs starting with **N** are new in round 1. IDs starting with **R** (retained) are round-0 IDs that survive with their original content. **S** IDs are the per-claim splits of a bundled round-0 item.\n\n### CHAPTER 1B — Nuclear and Existential-Risk Responsibility\n\n**Round-0 verdict preserved:** `no_discriminating_opportunity`. Zero defensible evidence; zero independent source families.\n\n**Round-1 search additions and outcome:**\n- \"Indonesia 2023 NPT nuclear nonproliferation disarmament position IAEA\" — repeatedly rate-limited upstream; the targeted queries were abandoned per the reviewer-brief note that this chapter is \"sufficiently closed on a documented no-discriminating-opportunity basis.\"\n- IISS, Springer, BTI 2026, Asia Maior Vol XXXV, East Asia Forum 2023 dossier searched for any 2023 nuclear-risk-bearing choice: **none surfaced.**\n- Targeted check: Indonesia's nuclear posture in 2023 is that of a non-nuclear-weapon state under the NPT (signatory since 1970), with small civilian research reactors at Serpong and Yogyakarta-Gajah Mada under IAEA safeguards. No expansion decision, no safeguards reform, no nuclear rhetoric in any 2023 conflict surfaced.\n\n**Round-1 retained items: 0.** No change.\n\n**Note on source-confidence registry:** Had any 2023 IAEA or disarmament source surfaced, the `OSCE/ODIHR`-style profiles would not apply; `official government sites` (Indonesian MFA) would be at `medium_low` for self-serving fairness claims. No candidate source reached the evidence-evaluation stage.\n\n**Rejection log (round 1):**\n- All web research calls for 1B: rejected because of repeated upstream rate-limiting and because the chapter guide's `no_discriminating_opportunity` disposition is already documented. Reason: `no_available_evidence_for_target_period_documented_no_discriminating_opportunity`.\n\n---\n\n### CHAPTER 2B — International Peace, Restraint, and Lawful Security\n\n**Round-0 estimate:** 3 defensible / 3 families. Reviewer issues: bundled sources, weak attribution, thin evidence with no saturation explanation.\n\n**Round-1 changes:** Decompose `IM23-A1` (which bundled ASEAN chair claim, 5PC document URL, and HRW position into one item). Add a counter-claim. Add a 2023-specific security posture anchor.\n\n#### 2B retained/restructured items\n\n| ID | Claim | URL / locator | Source type & conf. | Period fit | Ruler attribution evidence | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **R-2B-A** | Indonesia held the 2023 ASEAN chair. The chair's statement at the 32nd ACC/AMM Retreat (February 2023) \"contained only meek language\" on Myanmar, urging the junta to implement the Five-Point Consensus without concrete enforcement. | HRW (https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/03/31/indonesias-asean-leadership-tested-myanmar), paragraph beginning \"The chair's statement at the February Foreign Ministers' Retreat\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Feb 2023) | **Indirect** — Foreign Minister Retno Marsudi ran the chair; Jokowi set the strategic posture via cabinet-level direction. The mildness of the language is consistent with low-priority presidential direction. Ruler attribution is plausible but rests on inference. | `context` |\n| **R-2B-B** | HRW (March 2023): Indonesia established a low-profile \"special envoy's office\" headed by FM Marsudi (alongside diplomat Ngurah Swajaya) \"operating in a low key manner,\" rather than appointing an individual envoy like previous chairs; reported plans for a military envoy \"have not yet materialized.\" | HRW (same URL), paragraph \"Rather than appointing an individual as special envoy\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year | Indirect — choice of diplomatic instrument reflects presidential direction. **For ruler-attribution: positive but soft evidence** (Indonesian foreign policy is constitutionally a presidential domain). | `context` |\n| **N-2B-1** | ASEAN Leaders' Review and Decision on the Implementation of the Five-Point Consensus, 5 September 2023 — official ASEAN document. The PDF is available at the canonical ASEAN URL but the body text was not directly extractable in this worker session (PDF binary returned). Locator preserved as URL with `underlying_source_access_blocked` note. | https://asean.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/01.FINAL-ASEAN-LEADERS-REVIEW-AND-DECISION-ON-THE-IMPLEMENTATION-OF-THE-5PC-1.pdf | Official ASEAN document · context-only per registry | Target-year | Indirect — Jokowi signed as 2023 ASEAN chair | `context` (governs self-assessment only) |\n| **N-2B-2** | HRW World Report 2024 — Indonesia chapter, \"Key International Actors\" section: \"Indonesia chaired ASEAN in 2023, organizing multiple summits, but ASEAN did not press the military junta in Myanmar to implement any provisions of the Five Point Consensus achieved in Jakarta in April 2021 after the military coup in February 2021.\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, paragraph beginning \"Indonesia chaired ASEAN in 2023\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Indirect attribution to Jokowi as chair | `context` (for ruler-attribution) |\n| **N-2B-3** | East Asia Forum (Jan 2024, Siwage Dharma Negara / ISEAS retrospective on 2023): \"In 2023, Jokowi steered Indonesia through economic hurdles, fostered digital growth and addressed food inflation.\" ASEAN chairmanship and FIFA U-17 World Cup hosting listed among 2023 highlights. | https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/ opening paragraph | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Retrospective on 2023 | Direct: \"Jokowi steered Indonesia\" language | `context` (assessment language; not a specific decision) |\n| **N-2B-4** | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §3.1: \"Jokowi's foreign policy has been seen by the Indonesian public as more show than substance… Compared to his predecessor, Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono (SBY), Jokowi could boast few successes to match SBY's mediation of internal communal conflicts in Cambodia (border dispute with Thailand) and Southern Mindanao… Jokowi's interventions in Myanmar, targeted at finding a resolution to the political turmoil following the 1 February 2021 military coup and subsequent civil war, proved fruitless.\" | https://www.asiamaior.org/.../indonesia-2023-24...html, §3.1 | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | Retrospective on 2023 (cross-period) | Direct: scholarly judgment that Jokowi's foreign-policy interventions \"proved fruitless\" | `final_evidence` (independent scholarly assessment of 2023 conduct) |\n| **N-2B-5** | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §3.2 (Papua in foreign policy): \"Jokowi's initial promise of approaching the Papuan problem in a more humane manner was replaced by a more security-based approach with the police and army given the green light to intensify their military operations. Tight controls were also placed on news of gross violation of human rights and reports of the number of civilian deaths from the clashes between the OPM and the Indonesian security forces.\" | Asia Maior (same URL), §3.2 | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | Cross-period (covers 2023) | Direct: \"given the green light\" — explicit attribution of direction | `final_evidence` (independent attribution) |\n| **N-2B-6** *(counter-claim)* | HRW (March 2023): \"The foreign minister's frankness suggested Indonesia's chairmanship of ASEAN in 2023 would bring about a stronger regional response, following Cambodia's self-admitted foundering last year.\" The piece also documents Indonesia's behind-the-scenes UN Security Council engagement for an implementation plan with concrete indicators. | HRW (https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/03/31/indonesia-asean-leadership-tested-myanmar), paragraph \"Behind closed doors, Marsudi reportedly presented\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Feb–Mar 2023) | Indirect — Marsudi reported the UN plan; Jokowi's strategic posture was for diplomacy not coercion | `context` (positive-leaning counter-claim; useful for \"credible alternatives considered\" lens) |\n\n**2B dropped / reclassified:**\n- `IM23-A1` (round 0): **superseded** by R-2B-A + N-2B-1 + N-2B-2. Reason: bundled multiple sources and the ASEAN document URL.\n- `IM23-A2` (round 0): **superseded** by R-2B-A. Reason: same locator and claim.\n- `IM23-A3` (round 0): **superseded** by R-2B-B.\n- `IM23-A4` (round 0): **superseded** by N-2B-3 (better locator).\n- `IM23-A5` (round 0): **superseded** by N-2B-4 (more precise locator).\n\n**Chapter 2B filter ledger (round 1):** 6 source-claim units for 6 distinct sub-claims, 4 independent source organisations (HRW, East Asia Forum / ISEAS, Asia Maior, ASEAN Secretariat), 3 source types (NGO, academic-affiliated media, peer-reviewed journal) — independent of the official ASEAN document which is `context` per the registry.\n\n**Lens coverage (round 1):**\n- `2B.1` (diplomacy vs force): R-2B-A, R-2B-B, N-2B-2, N-2B-4, N-2B-6\n- `2B.2` (no wars of choice): no surfaced initiation; N-2B-5 documents intensified internal operations (Papua) which belong partly in 3B but recur here\n- `2B.3` (genuine defensive vs prestige): N-2B-3 (chairmanship as status), N-2B-5 (Papua security intensification with restrictive narrative control)\n- `2B.4` (civilian protection / IHL): thin; no Indonesian expeditionary 2023 conduct\n- `2B.5` (restrain proxies / allies): N-2B-5 (Papua); limited otherwise\n- `2B.6` (truthful threat explanation): N-2B-5 (controls on reporting); N-2B-6 (positive counter — Marsudi's UN engagement)\n- `2B.7` (peace processes): R-2B-A, R-2B-B, N-2B-2, N-2B-4, N-2B-6\n- `2B.8` (military spending for real security): LF001–LF004 contextual\n- `2B.9` (accountability): no major 2023 international accountability episode\n- `2B.10` (end-state change): N-2B-4 (regional environment unchanged; \"interventions… proved fruitless\")\n\n**Attribution statement (round 1):** The strongest direct Jokowi-attribution evidence in 2B is **N-2B-5** (\"the police and army given the green light to intensify their military operations\"), per an independent scholarly source. The weakest is the routine assumption that foreign policy is presidential in Indonesia's constitutional system. Items carrying **only** that constitutional presumption are kept as `context` for ruler attribution; items with an explicit attribution verb (\"steered\", \"given the green light\", \"interventions proved fruitless\") are kept as `final_evidence`.\n\n**Saturation note (round 1):** Indonesian 2023 international conduct beyond the ASEAN chair is genuinely thin (no wars of choice, no crises requiring direct presidential command, no NATO-equivalent commitments). The reviewer-brief worry that the chapter \"reports thin evidence but does not identify a credible saturation or access-blocker explanation\" is now addressed: **the thinness is consistent with the absence of an international conflict or crisis where Jokowi's personal direction was a discriminating factor. The principal live 2B question — what did Indonesia's ASEAN chair actually achieve on Myanmar — has been searched across at least four independent organisations; further search is unlikely to surface additional primary conduct.**\n\n---\n\n### CHAPTER 3B — Domestic Safety, Restraint, and Protection\n\n**Round-0 estimate:** 6 defensible / 3 families. Reviewer issues: subordinate conduct attributed to Jokowi without direction evidence; non-state violence treated as ruler-positive; ministerial SLAPP treated as Jokowi-attributed.\n\n**Round-1 changes:** Decompose bundled items, separate ruler-direction claims from pattern claims, add counter-claims, surface the Papua Paniai acquittal's temporal separation, and add the January 2023 Jokowi statement on past rights abuses (a direct ruler statement that establishes partial admission-without-apology, useful for the truthfulness/oversight lens).\n\n#### 3B retained/restructured items\n\n| ID | Claim | URL / locator | Source type & conf. | Period fit | Ruler attribution evidence | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **R-3B-D1** | Freedom House 2024 (covering 2023): \"Deadly confrontations involving security forces remain common in the Papua region, and have increased in geographic scope, intensity, and frequency since 2018. Security forces have been implicated in torture and extrajudicial killings, particularly in Papua.\" | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section F3 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year coverage | **Indirect** — chain of command assigns ultimate authority to president; but no evidence in this item of presidential direction. Keep as `context`. | `context` |\n| **R-3B-D2** | Freedom House 2024: \"In August 2023, Human Rights Monitor said that security forces' conduct in 2021 operations in Papua may amount to crimes against humanity.\" This is **2023 reporting on 2021 conduct** — temporal separation is required. | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section F3, paragraph beginning \"In August 2023\" | NGO · medium_high | Reporting date 2023; underlying conduct 2021 | **Indirect / retrospective** — the 2023 reporting exists, but the conduct it characterises predates the target period. Treat as `context`. | `context` (period-drift labelled) |\n| **R-3B-D3** | Freedom House 2024: in October 2023 an armed Papuan group attacked miners in Yahukimo Regency, killing at least 13 people — the worst attack on civilians by Papuan separatists since 2018. | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section F3 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Oct 2023) | **Non-state actor** — this item by itself does not bear on ruler performance. It does establish the security environment against which protection duty should be measured. Keep as `context`. | `context` |\n| **S-3B-D5a** | HRW World Report 2024: \"In December 2022, a special court convened in Makassar acquitted an army officer, Isak Sattu, of charges related to an infamous 2014 massacre in West Papua during which soldiers fired on hundreds of protesting Papuans… killing 4 teenagers and injuring as many as 21 other people, including women and children.\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Papua Provinces\" | NGO · medium_high | Pre-period acquittal (Dec 2022); referenced in 2023 reporting | **Indirect — impunity outcome**, but acquittal occurred before 2023; keep as `context`. | `context` |\n| **S-3B-D5b** *(split)* | HRW World Report 2024: \"In April, prominent human rights defenders Haris Azhar and Fatia Maulidiyanti were placed on trial in Jakarta for criminal defamation pursuant to a complaint by Luhut Binsar Pandjaitan, a senior minister in President Jokowi's cabinet, under Indonesia's Electronic Information and Transactions Law (ITE Law), relating to statements they made about Pandjaitan's alleged involvement in a gold mining project in West Papua.\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Papua Provinces\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (April 2023) | **Ministerial conduct** — the complaint was filed by Luhut (cabinet member). No evidence in this item of Jokowi's personal direction. Keep as `context` for ruler attribution; it is **direct** for institutional environment. | `context` (ruler-attribution) — but **retained** for institutional/political-freedom chapter (4B) as direct ministerial evidence |\n| **S-3B-D5c** *(split)* | HRW World Report 2024: \"Dozens of Papuans arrested for their participation in widespread anti-racism protests across Papua in 2019, including Malvin Yobe and Victor Yeimo, were released in 2023 after serving sentences. Authorities continue to arrest and prosecute Indigenous Papuans for expressing views in support of peaceful self-determination.\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Papua Provinces\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | **Mixed** — release of long-held prisoners is positive (potential state remedy); continued prosecution of peaceful self-determination expression is negative. No evidence of presidential direction either way. | `context` |\n| **R-3B-D6** | HRW World Report 2024: in March 2023, Komnas Perempuan held its first-ever hearing on mandatory dress regulations; female students, teachers, and parents testified to widespread bullying of those refusing to wear the hijab or jilbab. Many non-Muslims continue to face expulsion or pressure to withdraw from school. | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Women's and Girls' Rights\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (March 2023) | **Indirect** — Komnas Perempuan is an independent commission; school-level enforcement is local. No evidence of presidential direction. | `context` |\n| **R-3B-D7** | HRW World Report 2024: the government designated \"armed criminal groups\" (Papuan armed groups) as terrorist organisations in 2021; in 2023 authorities continued to arrest and prosecute Indigenous Papuans for expressing views in support of peaceful self-determination. | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Papua Provinces\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | **Indirect** — institutional pattern; no presidential direction evidence. | `context` |\n| **S-3B-D8a** | HRW World Report 2024: \"In July 2023, in an important step forward, the Constitutional Court partially granted [a petition by Indonesian Mental Health Association] by altering the nature of guardianship of people with disabilities from being mandatory to being optional.\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Disability Rights\" | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (July 2023) | **Independent judiciary** — not presidential direction. But a positive rights outcome in 2023. | `context` |\n| **S-3B-D8b** | HRW World Report 2024: \"UN Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities called on the Indonesian government to eradicate restrictive practices such as pasung (shackling). According to a recent media report, seven people with psychosocial disabilities (mental health conditions) who were shackled died on Flores Island between June and September [2023].\" | https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia, section \"Disability Rights\" | NGO + UN treaty body · medium_high | Target-year (Jun–Sep 2023) | **Indirect** — local practice; no evidence of presidential direction. | `context` |\n| **R-3B-D9** | Freedom House 2024: in January 2023, **Jokowi** acknowledged 12 major human rights abuses committed by the government, including the 1965–66 massacres, and promised compensation, though he did not offer a formal apology. Papuan activists criticised the government, saying it did not sufficiently acknowledge abuses committed against Papuans. | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section F4 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Jan 2023) | **Direct** — Jokowi's own statement. | `final_evidence` (direct ruler statement; partial acknowledgment without apology) |\n| **S-3B-D10a** | Freedom House 2024: in September 2023, Rempang Island protests — Indonesian authorities sought to evict several thousand residents for the Rempang Eco-City development; protesters and authorities clashed at a demonstration. **Jokowi reversed course on forcibly evicting residents after the clashes**, but residents reported that officials were subsequently pressuring them to accept a relocation offer. | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, sections D4 / G2 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Sept 2023) | **Direct** — Jokowi's reversal is attributed to him explicitly. Positive-leaning for restraint under pressure; negative-leaning because the underlying relocation pressure continued. | `final_evidence` (direct ruler action) |\n| **N-3B-1** | Freedom House 2024: \"An explosive device was detonated outside the home of Papuan journalist Victor Mambor in January [2023]. An investigation identified no suspects before it was closed.\" | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section D1 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Jan 2023) | **Indirect** — incident + investigation failure. No evidence of presidential direction or remedy. | `context` |\n| **N-3B-2** | Freedom House 2024: \"In 2021, the government designated 'armed criminal groups,' the government moniker used for armed Papuan groups, as terrorist organizations, granting law enforcement greater detention powers and more resources.\" (Carried forward as 2023 institutional context.) | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section F3 | NGO · medium_high | Cross-period; reference persists in 2023 | Indirect | `context` |\n| **N-3B-3** *(counter-claim)* | BTI 2026 Indonesia report: \"Among the more positive political developments during the period under review were **the absence of massive human rights violations** and — except for unrest in the Papua provinces — the general conditions of political stability and reduced political polarization.\" | https://bti-project.org/en/reports/country-report/IDN, summary paragraph | Academic index · medium_high | Period-fit (cross-period) | Indirect scholarly assessment | `context` (positive counter-claim to the security-force lens) |\n| **N-3B-4** *(counter-claim)* | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §3.2: \"Despite Jokowi's 2014 campaign promise to achieve a political resolution of the long-running Papuan problem during his first incumbency (2014-2019), human rights abuses in the troubled half-island continued.\" Identifies that despite the security turn, the abuse pattern is largely inheritance-plus-continuation, not 2023-original. | Asia Maior (https://www.asiamaior.org/.../indonesia-2023-24...html), §3.2 | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | Cross-period | Indirect (assessment of inheritance) | `context` |\n\n**3B dropped / reclassified:**\n- `IM23-D1` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D1.\n- `IM23-D2` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D2 (with explicit period-drift label).\n- `IM23-D3` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D3 (explicitly marked non-state actor).\n- `IM23-D4` (round 0): **superseded** by S-3B-D5a (period drift) and S-3B-D5c (release).\n- `IM23-D5` (round 0): **superseded** by S-3B-D5b (now flagged as ministerial complaint, not presidential direction; cross-referenced to 4B).\n- `IM23-D6` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D6.\n- `IM23-D7` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D7 + N-3B-2.\n- `IM23-D8` (round 0): **superseded** by S-3B-D8a + S-3B-D8b (split into positive court outcome and negative ongoing practice).\n- `IM23-D9` (round 0): **superseded** by R-3B-D9 (preserved as `final_evidence` for direct Jokowi statement).\n- `IM23-D10` (round 0): **superseded** by S-3B-D10a (Rempang, with explicit Jokowi reversal attribution).\n- `IM23-D11` (round 0): **superseded** by N-3B-3.\n\n**Chapter 3B filter ledger (round 1):** 14 source-claim units (R-3B-D1, R-3B-D2, R-3B-D3, S-3B-D5a, S-3B-D5b, S-3B-D5c, R-3B-D6, R-3B-D7, S-3B-D8a, S-3B-D8b, R-3B-D9, S-3B-D10a, N-3B-1, N-3B-2, N-3B-3, N-3B-4 = 16 items actually — under the 20-unit ceiling). Of these, only **2** carry direct Jokowi attribution (`final_evidence`): R-3B-D9 (January 2023 admission) and S-3B-D10a (Rempang reversal). The remaining 14 are `context` (institutional, ministerial, security-force, or inherited pattern).\n\n**Lens coverage (round 1):**\n- `3B.1` (state violence): R-3B-D1, R-3B-D7, S-3B-D5a (Papua conduct pattern); R-3B-D9 (admission without apology)\n- `3B.2` (security-force abuse): R-3B-D1, R-3B-D2, R-3B-D7, S-3B-D5a, S-3B-D5b\n- `3B.3` (incitement): no direct 2023 incitement statement surfaced\n- `3B.4` (oversight / remedy): R-3B-D9 (partial admission), S-3B-D5a (impunity), S-3B-D8a (court partial grant), S-3B-D8b (shackling deaths)\n- `3B.5` (emergency / surveillance): R-3B-D7 (Papuan-group designation)\n- `3B.6` (fear reduction without state terror): N-3B-3 (BTI positive), N-3B-4 (inheritance note)\n- `3B.7` (vulnerable groups): R-3B-D6 (hijab), S-3B-D8b (pasung), R-3B-D3 (non-state violence against civilians)\n- `3B.8` (peaceful protest): S-3B-D10a (Rempang reversal positive), S-3B-D5c (continued prosecution negative), R-3B-D7 (prosecution negative)\n- `3B.9` (crisis response): S-3B-D10a\n- `3B.10` (end-state change): N-3B-3 (BTI stability note), N-3B-4 (inheritance), R-3B-D9 (admission as remedy partial)\n\n**Attribution statement (round 1):** The reviewer is right that \"countrywide and subordinate security-force conduct is repeatedly linked to Jokowi without evidence of direction, knowledge, tolerance, correction, or authority-based responsibility.\" Round 1 distinguishes those items that carry an explicit attribution verb (S-3B-D10a: \"Jokowi reversed course\"; R-3B-D9: \"Jokowi acknowledged\") from those that describe a pattern at the institutional or subordinate level. Items in the second category remain in the ledger because they are the most material 2023 facts about safety in Indonesia, but they are demoted to `context` for the ruler-attribution question.\n\n**Saturation note (round 1):** The reviewer asked for \"direct presidential or executive accountability for Papua security conduct,\" \"2023 civilian-casualty and protection events,\" and \"security-force investigation, discipline, and remedy outcomes.\" The first remains a genuine gap: no 2023 source surfaced in which Jokowi issued a direct order, public reprimand, or policy reversal specifically on Papua security-force conduct. The second is partly addressed (Yahukimo October 2023 attack; Victor Mambor January 2023 device). The third is partly addressed (Paniai 2022 acquittal carried forward; no 2023 discipline event surfaced). Further round-2 search is unlikely to change the attribution picture; the inheritance-plus-continuation framing from N-3B-4 is the strongest available.\n\n---\n\n### CHAPTER 4B — Political Freedom vs Authoritarian Rule\n\n**Round-0 estimate:** 3 defensible / 3 families. Reviewer issues: bundled sources, weak attribution, retrospective 2024 evidence mixed with 2023, ministerial conduct mis-attributed, asserted reuse not demonstrated.\n\n**Round-1 changes:** Split IM23-P1 (the bundled Constitutional Court ruling item) into the ruling itself, the recusal/conflict-of-interest detail, and the ethics-panel aftermath. Split IM23-P3 and IM23-P11 into 2023-anchor sub-claims and 2024-only sub-claims, retaining only the 2023-anchored ones for ruler attribution. Add direct EAF articles on the dynasty and PDI-P tension (which document Jokowi's strategic posture in 2023 electioneering decisions, with mixed valence). Add a counter-claim noting that PDI-P–related internal party dynamics complicated the dynasty narrative. Reclassify IM23-P9 to `context` for ruler attribution (ministerial complaint).\n\n#### 4B retained/restructured items\n\n| ID | Claim | URL / locator | Source type & conf. | Period fit | Ruler attribution evidence | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **S-4B-P1a** *(split)* | AP News (16 Oct 2023): \"Indonesia's top court on Monday ruled against lowering the minimum age of presidential and vice presidential candidates as the country gears up for election in 2024, but allowed an exception for those who served or been elected as regional leaders allowing them to run at younger age.\" Ruling 7-to-2; final. | https://apnews.com/article/indonesia-constitutional-court-minimum-age-presidential-candidate-6a610ea14a4c6f4070c0bf68078f5a72 | Major wire · medium_high | Target-year (16 Oct 2023) | **Indirect** — the ruling is by the Constitutional Court, not by Jokowi. Court independence is the relevant attribution frame. | `final_evidence` (court decision; targets a 2023 event) |\n| **S-4B-P1b** *(split)* | AP News (16 Oct 2023): \"The ruling could pave the way for President Joko Widodo's eldest son, Gibran Rakabuming Raka, 36, to run in the election next year… Chief Justice Anwar Usman said the article in the General Election Law in full would read candidates at least 40 years old 'or those who served or currently holding a position as regional leader through the elections.'\" **Usman did not recuse despite being Jokowi's brother-in-law and Gibran's uncle.** | Same AP News URL, paragraphs beginning \"The ruling could pave the way\" and \"Chief Justice Anwar Usman said\" | Major wire · medium_high | Target-year | **Indirect** — court conduct; family relationship documented but no evidence of presidential direction. The non-recusal is a court-internal failure. | `final_evidence` (court-internal; factual) |\n| **S-4B-P1c** *(split)* | AP News (16 Oct 2023): \"One of them [the four dissenting judges], Judge Saldi Isra questioned the reason behind the ruling. He said that during his decades-long career, 'this is the first time' to have experienced such a ruling.\" Dominique Nicky Fahrizal (CSIS): \"the public will consider the Constitutional Court as an instrument that can be controlled by those in power who wish to legitimize a particular political agenda.\" | Same AP News URL, paragraph beginning \"One of them\" and the analyst quote | Major wire · medium_high | Target-year | Mixed — judicial dissent + analyst assessment; indirect attribution | `final_evidence` (factual dissent; analyst quote is `context`) |\n| **N-4B-1** | Al Jazeera (16 Oct 2023): \"The Constitutional Court rules the minimum age for candidates remains 40, and any change should come from lawmakers.\" Corroborating report