to Jokowi for 2023 conduct) |\n| **S-4B-P3b** *(split, 2024-only, demoted)* | BTI 2026 Indonesia report: \"The 2024 presidential election was widely described as the most unfair and rigged since the end of the authoritarian New Order in 1998. The Prabowo-Gibran ticket received massive support from the state apparatus…\" | Same URL | Academic index · medium_high | **2024 election period**, not 2023 | Not 2023-attributable | `context` (period-drift labelled; relevant as retrospective on 2023 setup but not as 2023 conduct) |\n| **S-4B-P5** | EAF (Yoes Kenawas, 22 Oct 2023): \"Just three days after he became a member of the Indonesian Solidarity Party (PSI) in September 2023, Indonesian President Joko 'Jokowi' Widodo's youngest son, Kaesang Pangarep, was appointed the party's chairman… Less than three weeks later, the Indonesian public was again shocked by a Constitutional Court decision to exempt candidates with experience as elected officials from a legal rule barring those under 40 from competing in presidential elections… These twin events mark the emergence of a Jokowi dynasty, as the president prepares to leave office in 2024.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/22/jokowis-dynasty-building-risks-tensions-with-his-pdi-p-party-base/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (Sep–Oct 2023) | **Direct** (the piece describes the dynasty as emerging from these specific 2023 events). | `final_evidence` (direct 2023 attribution) |\n| **S-4B-P5b** | EAF (22 Oct 2023): \"As a PDI-P cadre, Jokowi has expressed his support for the party's presidential candidate, former governor of Central Java Ganjar Pranowo. But his frequent appearances alongside Prabowo and the moves by some Jokowi volunteer groups to endorse Prabowo seem to substantiate speculation that the president actually favours his defence minister's candidacy.\" | Same EAF URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | **Direct** (describes Jokowi's 2023 campaign-period positioning). | `final_evidence` |\n| **N-4B-3** | EAF Editorial Board (23 Oct 2023): \"Jokowi, initially sceptical of his son's electoral prospects on the national stage, and wary of accusations of nepotism, came to embrace the idea with the zeal of the convert.\" The piece documents the political logic of Gibran's candidacy. | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/23/jokowi-takes-a-huge-chance-with-sons-political-elevation/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | **Direct** | `final_evidence` |\n| **N-4B-4** | EAF Editorial Board (23 Oct 2023): \"After a dramatic weekend of political manoeuvring, Prabowo announced that his party coalition had backed his choice of Gibran as his running mate and that they would formally register their candidacy on 25 October.\" Snap opinion polls were commissioned by Jokowi and the Prabowo campaign to gauge backlash depth. | Same URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (Oct 2023) | **Direct** (commissioning of polls is a Jokowi-attributable action). | `final_evidence` |\n| **R-4B-P6** | Freedom House 2024: \"In 2023, the DPR passed amendments to the UU ITE… The amended UU ITE also prohibits expression that fosters 'feelings of hatred or hostility' for protected individuals. The amended law was not signed by the president by year's end.\" | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section D4 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Dec 2023) | **Direct** — non-signature is documented as a Jokowi decision/action. | `final_evidence` |\n| **S-4B-P7a** *(split)* | Freedom House 2024: in September 2023, Rempang Island protests — protesters opposing a development project clashed with security forces, who used tear gas and water cannon; **at least 40 people were arrested.** | https://freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024, section D4 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Sept 2023) | **Indirect** — security-force conduct; no evidence of presidential direction of the crackdown. Keep as `context`. | `context` |\n| **S-4B-P7b** *(split)* | Freedom House 2024 (G2): \"Jokowi reversed course on forcibly evicting residents after protesters and authorities clashed at a demonstration that month, but residents reported that officials were subsequently pressuring them to accept a relocation offer.\" | Same URL, section G2 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Sept 2023) | **Direct** — Jokowi reversal. | `final_evidence` (cross-referenced to 3B S-3B-D10a) |\n| **N-4B-5** | Freedom House 2024: \"an explosive device was detonated outside the home of Papuan journalist Victor Mambor in January [2023]; an investigation identified no suspects before it was closed.\" (Carried for media-environment lens.) | Same URL, section D1 | NGO · medium_high | Target-year (Jan 2023) | Indirect | `context` |\n| **N-4B-6** *(counter-claim — contestability positive)* | EAF Editorial Board (23 Oct 2023): \"Political dynasticism is more generally on the rise, though, by regional standards, from a low base… So far, Indonesian political dynasties have mostly remained localised and highly vulnerable to defeat by non-dynastic rivals. Indonesia is a long way from the situation in the Philippines, for instance, where political clans centred on powerful families have long been the main unit of political organisation…\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/23/jokowi-takes-a-huge-chance-with-sons-political-elevation/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year | Indirect scholarly assessment | `context` (positive-leaning counter-claim: contestation is still real in Indonesia) |\n| **N-4B-7** *(counter-claim — PDI-P tension)* | EAF (22 Oct 2023): \"Jokowi and PDI-P are locked in a mutual hostage situation. Jokowi still needs PDI-P to ensure his government's stability in his final year in office. On the other hand, PDI-P needs Jokowi as an electoral magnet for the February 2024 legislative elections.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/22/jokowis-dynasty-building-risks-tensions-with-his-pdi-p-party-base/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year | Indirect scholarly assessment | `context` (positive-leaning counter-claim: executive is not unilateral; PDI-P retains leverage) |\n\n**4B dropped / reclassified:**\n- `IM23-P1` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P1a + S-4B-P1b + S-4B-P1c + N-4B-1 + N-4B-2.\n- `IM23-P2` (round 0): **superseded** by R-4B-P2.\n- `IM23-P3` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P3a (kept) + S-4B-P3b (demoted to `context`, period-drift labelled).\n- `IM23-P4` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P5 + S-4B-P5b + N-4B-3 + N-4B-4.\n- `IM23-P5` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P5 + S-4B-P5b + N-4B-7.\n- `IM23-P6` (round 0): **superseded** by R-4B-P6 (better locator).\n- `IM23-P7` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P7a (context, no presidential direction) + S-4B-P7b (direct Jokowi reversal).\n- `IM23-P8` (round 0): **superseded** by N-4B-5.\n- `IM23-P9` (round 0): **superseded** by S-3B-D5b (re-typed for ruler attribution; primary chapter for the SLAPP pattern is 4B but the attribution is ministerial).\n- `IM23-P10` (round 0): **dropped** in round 1. Reason: 2024 RSF index data, post-target-period. The 2023 RSF rank (LF025) remains as local prior context. Disposition: `rejected` with reason `period_drift_index_published_2024_only`.\n- `IM23-P11` (round 0): **superseded** by S-4B-P3b (demoted to `context`, period-drift labelled) and the remaining pattern claims (KPU salary rise, etc.) explicitly excluded as 2024-attributable.\n\n**Chapter 4B filter ledger (round 1):** 17 source-claim units (S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, N-4B-1, N-4B-2, R-4B-P2, S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5, S-4B-P5b, N-4B-3, N-4B-4, R-4B-P6, S-4B-P7a, S-4B-P7b, N-4B-5, N-4B-6, N-4B-7) across 5 independent source organisations (AP News, Al Jazeera, ConstitutionNet, Freedom House, EAF). Under the 20-unit ceiling.\n\n**Lens coverage (round 1):**\n- `4B.1` (electoral contestability): S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5, N-4B-3, N-4B-4, N-4B-6 (counter-claim)\n- `4B.2` (manipulation of rules): S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, S-4B-P3a\n- `4B.3` (tolerance of opposition): S-3B-D5b (ministerial complaint), S-4B-P7b (Rempang reversal positive)\n- `4B.4` (independent courts): S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, R-4B-P2 (ethics remedy)\n- `4B.5` (avoid personality cults): S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5, N-4B-4\n- `4B.6` (independent media): N-4B-5 (Mambor), R-4B-P6 (UU ITE amendments not signed), S-3B-D5b (SLAPP)\n- `4B.7` (political equality): N-4B-7 (PDI-P leverage retained — counter-claim); no 2023 affirmative action surfaced\n- `4B.8` (term limits / succession): S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5\n- `4B.9` (surveillance / digital controls): R-4B-P6\n- `4B.10` (trajectory): S-4B-P3a, N-4B-3, N-4B-6 (counter-claim), N-4B-7 (counter-claim), S-4B-P3b (period-drift)\n\n**Attribution statement (round 1):** Items S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5, S-4B-P5b, N-4B-3, N-4B-4, R-4B-P6, S-4B-P7b carry **direct** Jokowi attribution for 2023 conduct. The court-internal items (S-4B-P1a–c, N-4B-1, N-4B-2, R-4B-P2) are court decisions that affect the political-freedom lens but are not presidential direction; they are `final_evidence` as facts, not as ruler-attributed conduct.\n\n**Saturation note (round 1):** The reviewer asked for \"evidence of Jokowi's direction, knowledge, benefit, or tolerance regarding Constitutional Court manipulation.\" Round 1 has surfaced: the family relationship (S-4B-P1b), the implicit political logic (N-4B-3, N-4B-4, S-4B-P5), the dynasty framing (S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5), and the PDI-P tension counter (N-4B-7). What has **not** surfaced is direct documentary evidence (e.g., a phone recording, a leaked instruction, a court clerk testimony) of Jokowi personally directing Justice Usman. Further search is unlikely to surface such direct evidence; the strongest available 2023 attribution rests on inference from the documented family relationship and the documented political benefit.\n\n---\n\n### CHAPTER 5B — Economic Well-Being and Prosperity\n\n**Round-0 estimate:** 4 defensible / 3 families. Reviewer issues: bundled programs, BI independence mis-attributed, late-period poverty data, 2021–22 evidence, lack of distribution/health/education 2023 evidence.\n\n**Round-1 changes:** Split bundled items. Add a 2023-specific poverty/inflation macro anchor from EAF and BPS-cited material. Add an independent IMF Article IV (June 2023) reference (N-5B-1) that the regulator-adjacent coverage can use. Add a counter-claim (N-5B-7) on the political economy of the downstreaming policy. Reclassify BI rate-hike to `context` (independent central bank).\n\n#### 5B retained/restructured items\n\n| ID | Claim | URL / locator | Source type & conf. | Period fit | Ruler attribution evidence | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **S-5B-E1a** *(split)* | EAF (Jan 2024): \"The government spent 177.5 trillion rupiah (US$11.4 billion) on People's Business Credit, a subsidy program to support small-scale entrepreneurs, like street food vendors, amid rising interest rates.\" | https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Direct (government spending) | `final_evidence` |\n| **S-5B-E1b** *(split)* | EAF (Jan 2024): \"El Nino conditions disrupted food production, driving up food inflation, especially rice prices. The government responded with price stabilisation policies and social assistance… an additional 2.7 trillion Indonesian rupiah (US$173.2 million) in food assistance and 7.5 trillion rupiah (US$481.5 million) in direct cash assistance to poor households.\" | Same URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Direct (social-assistance disbursement) | `final_evidence` |\n| **R-5B-E2** | EAF (Dec 2023 / East Asia Forum Quarterly \"Industrial policy 2.0\" Vol 15 No 4): \"Almost US$14 billion has been invested in nickel smelter capacity in Indonesia. Maluku Utara and Sulawesi Tengah, Indonesia's nickel downstreaming provinces, experienced double-digit growth rates in 2021, driven primarily by investment in the industry. Jokowi has highlighted how the ban has seen a 30-fold increase in the value of Indonesia's nickel-related exports.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/12/07/indonesia-doubles-down-on-nickel-export-bans-and-downstreaming/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Direct (Jokowi's framing of the downstreaming program) | `final_evidence` |\n| **R-5B-E3** *(reclassified)* | EAF (Jan 2024): \"Bank Indonesia increased its policy interest rate to 6 per cent to defend the domestic currency and maintain price stability. Interest rates, together with other fiscal policy tools, were used to support small businesses.\" | https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | **Independence caveat** — Bank Indonesia is formally independent. The rate decision should not be attributed to the executive. Re-type to `context` for ruler attribution, retain as `context`. | `context` |\n| **N-5B-1** | EAF (28 Sep 2023): \"The International Monetary Fund's June 2023 assessment of Indonesia's export ban policy has reignited debate on Indonesia's downstream industrial policy.\" Cites IMF Article IV staff report. | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/09/28/assessing-nickel-downstreaming-in-indonesia/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (June 2023) | Indirect (IMF assessment of executive policy) | `context` (per source-confidence registry, IMF is institutional; good for context) |\n| **N-5B-2** | EAF (28 Sep 2023): \"In 2014 Indonesia banned the export of nickel ore. The policy has been deemed a success, with exports of ferronickel and stainless steel increasing from US$2.2 billion in 2014 to US$29 billion in 2022.\" Confirms the macro trajectory. | Same URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Cross-period (2023-published) | Indirect | `context` |\n| **N-5B-3** | EAF (14 Oct 2023): \"In 2020, Indonesia banned the export of raw nickel ore to attract investment, largely in nickel smelters. A year later, the country received downstream investments and commitments from Chinese companies totalling some US$30 billion. As of July 2023, there were already 43 nickel smelters operating, 28 under construction and 24 in the planning stage.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/14/reassessing-indonesias-nickel-downstreaming-policy/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (Jul 2023 smelter count) | Indirect (program execution) | `context` |\n| **N-5B-4** | EAF (7 Dec 2023): \"In terms of attracting investment, Indonesia's nickel downstreaming policies have produced results… About 90 per cent of the country's nickel processing facilities are dominated by Chinese companies. Prominent players include Tsingshan Holding Group, Zhejiang Huayou Cobalt, Ningbo Lygend (part of CATL Group), Wuling Motors and China Molybdenum Company. But the growing presence of large South Korean conglomerates — such as Hyundai Motors, LG Energy and SK — and Taiwan's Foxconn have somewhat tempered China's dominance.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/12/07/indonesia-doubles-down-on-nickel-export-bans-and-downstreaming/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Indirect | `context` |\n| **N-5B-5** | EAF (14 Oct 2023): \"the country's over-reliance on China for investment and market access is a concern. About 90 per cent of the country's nickel processing facilities are dominated by Chinese companies.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/14/reassessing-indonesias-nickel-downstreaming-policy/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Indirect (programme structure) | `context` |\n| **N-5B-6** | EAF (7 Dec 2023): \"The fact that the European Union took legal action against Indonesia over the nickel export ban and won with US support does not help.\" (i.e., WTO ruling against Indonesia.) | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/12/07/indonesia-doubles-down-on-nickel-export-bans-and-downstreaming/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (WTO ruling) | Indirect | `final_evidence` (international accountability event) |\n| **N-5B-7** *(counter-claim)* | EAF (14 Oct 2023): \"the success of the nickel downstreaming policy is in part due to Indonesia being the world's largest nickel producer with the largest reserves. This might not hold true for other minerals and commodities, calling for more careful application across different sectors.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/10/14/reassessing-indonesias-nickel-downstreaming-policy/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year | Indirect scholarly caveat | `context` (counter-claim on replicability) |\n| **R-5B-E4** | EAF (Jan 2024): \"In 2023, Indonesia successfully held the ASEAN chairmanship and hosted the FIFA U-17 World Cup… Indonesia also launched its first high-speed railway 'Whoosh' connecting Jakarta and Bandung, with a planned extension to Surabaya.\" | https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year | Direct (flagship launch) | `final_evidence` (cross-ref 8B) |\n| **R-5B-E8** | BTI 2026 Indonesia (paraphrased): \"Indonesia's GDP growth rate has returned to pre-pandemic levels, and stable growth of around 5% is expected in the near future. Commodity exports to countries such as India and China are booming.\" | https://bti-project.org/en/reports/country-report/IDN, summary | Academic index · medium_high | Period-fit (covers 2023) | Indirect (macro outcome) | `context` |\n| **N-5B-8** | EAF (Jan 2024 retrospective): \"Finance Minister Sri Mulyani stated that in 2023 Indonesia's digital economy would surge to US$82 billion, marking an 8 per cent increase from 2022. Mulyani noted that online commerce would continue to be the primary catalyst for digital sector expansion.\" | https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Direct (minister's statement attributable to executive programme) | `context` |\n| **N-5B-9** | EAF (Jan 2024): \"Healthy domestic consumption is reflected by continued growth in the digital economy, particularly in the e-commerce sector.\" | Same URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Target-year (2023) | Indirect | `context` |\n| **S-5B-E5a** *(split, retyped)* | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §2.8: \"By the end of the Jokowi presidency, gross national income per-capita continued to be stuck below US$ 4,000 (US$ 3,953 in 2023).\" | Asia Maior Vol XXXV (URL above) | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | Cross-period (2023 figure cited) | Indirect (outcome) | `context` |\n| **S-5B-E5b** *(split, demoted)* | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §2.8: \"the percentage of the 280 million Indonesian population living below the International Poverty Line (IPL) of US$ 1.90 per day, as of September 2024, was just under 9% (8.56).\" | Same URL | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | **2024 data point**, not 2023 | Not 2023-attributable | `rejected` with reason `period_drift_poverty_data_september_2024` |\n| **S-5B-E6a** *(split, retyped)* | Asia Maior Vol XXXV 2024, §2.5: \"in the penultimate two years of Jokowi's incumbency (2021-2022) the budget for his core policy, infrastructure development, increased by 22.2% to US$ 30 billion, while spending on education fell 4% from 17.94 to 13.93%.\" | Same URL, §2.5 | Peer-reviewed area-studies journal · medium_high | **2021-22 data**, not 2023 | Not 2023-attributable | `context` (relevant to inherited-baseline lens) |\n| **N-5B-10** | EAF (10 Aug 2023): \"Data from Indonesia's Investment Coordinating Body, BKPM, analysed by the Jakarta think tank IDEAS shows that investment in minerals processing was US$7.8 billion in total between 2010 and 2014, rising to US$15.8 billion between 2015 and 2019. Between 2020, when a ban on nickel exports came into full effect, and 2022 there has been more than US$23 billion investment in minerals processing, most of it from China and Hong Kong.\" | https://www.eastasiaforum.org/2023/08/14/jokowinomics-headed-for-a-third-term/ | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Cross-period (covers 2023 execution) | Indirect (programme execution data) | `context` |\n| **N-5B-11** | EAF (10 Aug 2023): \"Even the IMF, while encouraging a phase-out of export bans and a warning of the distortionary effects that export bans impose on global markets, estimates that about two-thirds of the increased value of Indonesian nickel output can be attributed to government interventions rather than increased prices.\" | Same URL | Reputable academic-affiliated media · medium_high | Cross-period | Indirect | `context` |\n\n**5B dropped / reclassified:**\n- `IM23-E1` (round 0): **superseded** by S-5B-E1a (KUR small-business credit) + S-5B-E1b (food + cash assistance).\n- `IM23-E3` (round 0): **superseded** by R-5B-E3 (re-typed to `context` for ruler attribution).\n- `IM23-E5` (round 0): **superseded** by S-5B-E5a (kept, 2023 GNI per capita) + S-5B-E5b (rejected, 2024 poverty data).\n- `IM23-E6` (round 0): **superseded** by S-5B-E6a (2021–22 data re-typed as inherited-baseline `context`).\n- `IM23-E7` (round 0): **rejected** in round 1. Reason: the September 2024 PP 23/2023 amendment and the sand-export re-opening are post-period. The IKN and downstreaming execution facts that survive are now in R-5B-E4, N-5B-2/3/4/5/10/11.\n\n**Chapter 5B filter ledger (round 1):** 18 source-claim units (within 20-unit ceiling). **5** carry direct executive attribution (S-5B-E1a, S-5B-E1b, R-5B-E2, R-5B-E4, N-5B-8). 1 is a final-evidence international accountability event (N-5B-6, WTO ruling against Indonesia). 12 are `context` (programme execution, macro outcomes, IMF/think-tank assessments, inherited baselines).\n\n**Lens coverage (round 1):**\n- `5B.1` (broad prosperity intent): R-5B-E2, R-5B-E4, S-5B-E1a, S-5B-E1b\n- `5B.2` (competent appointments): no 2023 personnel-change evidence surfaced (search note below)\n- `5B.3` (macro stability): R-5B-E3 (rate hike, context), N-5B-11 (IMF share-of-output estimate)\n- `5B.4` (fair rules): N-5B-2, N-5B-5, N-5B-6 (WTO loss)\n- `5B.5` (anti-corruption / capture): cross-ref 7B (Firli case)\n- `5B.6` (productivity foundations): R-5B-E2, R-5B-E4, N-5B-2, N-5B-3, N-5B-4, S-5B-E6a (education-spend decline)\n- `5B.7` (evidence-based correction): N-5B-1 (IMF critique), N-5B-7 (counter-claim on replicability)\n- `5B.8` (fair distribution): S-5B-E1b (assistance to poor), S-5B-E5a (low GNI/capita), N-5B-5 (concentration on Chinese investors)\n- `5B.9` (shock management): S-5B-E1b (El Niño response)\n- `5B.10` (trajectory): R-5B-E8 (5% growth), N-5B-2 (export trajectory), N-5B-4 (investment), S-5B-E6a (education-spend decline), N-5B-10, N-5B-11\n\n**Attribution statement (round 1):** Items S-5B-E1a/b, R-5B-E2, R-5B-E4, N-5B-8 carry direct executive attribution for 2023. Items N-5B-1–7, N-5B-9–11 are `context` for ruler attribution. R-5B-E3 (BI rate hike) is `context` because Bank Indonesia is constitutionally independent.\n\n**Search limitation note (round 1):** A targeted search for 2023 personnel-change evidence (cabinet reshuffle, finance minister appointment, anti-corruption agency leadership change beyond the Firli dismissal) was attempted but did not surface a directly attributable 2023 cabinet decision. The Firli dismissal is a 2023 event covered in 7B.\n\n---\n\n### CHAPTER 6B — Social Well-Being and Human Development\n\n**Round-0 estimate:** 3 defensible / 3 families. Reviewer issues: locator mistake on IM23-S7 (Asia Maior source cited with BTI locator); bundled items; institutional outcomes attributed to Jokowi without 