rget), N-8B-2 (Luhut task force), N-8B-8 (cost estimate and quote)\n- `8B.3` (mobilisation / coordination): R-8B-F2, N-8B-4, N-8B-5 (SOE mobilisation), S-8B-F9a (Merauke military involvement), R-8B-F11 (digital economy)\n- `8B.4` (capable personnel): no 2023 personnel-change evidence surfaced\n- `8B.5` (internal discipline): R-8B-F2, N-8B-5, S-8B-F8a (BRIN dysfunction)\n- `8B.6` (observable implementation): S-8B-F1a (ASEAN + FIFA), S-8B-F1b (Whoosh), S-8B-F4b (NSP rollout), S-8B-F9a (Merauke)\n- `8B.7` (outcomes move in claimed direction): N-8B-3 (downstreaming success), N-8B-8 (IKN \"Indonesia-centric\" goal), R-8B-F3 (investor caution negative)\n- `8B.8` (adaptation): no direct 2023 corrective-action evidence surfaced; R-8B-F2 + N-8B-5 (debt problem acknowledged but corrective action not documented)\n- `8B.9` (crisis management): N-8B-6 (El Niño response), cross-ref 3B S-3B-D10a (Rempang reversal), cross-ref 7B N-7B-1 (Firli dismissal)\n- `8B.10` (trajectory): S-8B-F10a (democratic-recession framing), R-8B-F3 (investor caution), S-8B-F8a (BRIN failure), S-8B-F7a (5% growth), N-8B-7 (digital-economy counter-claim)\n\n**Attribution statement (round 1):** Round 1 establishes that the **IKN programme** (N-8B-1, N-8B-2, N-8B-8) and the **SOE-reliance programme** (R-8B-F2, N-8B-4, N-8B-5) carry the strongest direct Jokowi attribution for 2023 effectiveness. The **Whoosh launch** (S-8B-F1b) and the **ASEAN/FIFA hosting** (S-8B-F1a) are visible implementation outcomes. The **BRIN failure** (S-8B-F8a) carries direct Jokowi-attribution language. The remaining items are programme-execution or inherited-baseline.\n\n**Saturation note (round 1):** Round 1 has filled several reviewer-flagged gaps (corrective adaptation, IKN execution, SOE corrective responsibility, BRIN). The remaining weak lens is `8B.4` (capable personnel) and `8B.8` (adaptation): no 2023 personnel-change event (cabinet reshuffle, key replacement after failure) and no documented corrective action after a 2023 failure surfaced. Further search is unlikely to change this; the BRIN programme is the closest to a documented failure-without-correction item.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cross-Chapter Synthesis — Round 1\n\n### 3.1 Same-URL splits and reuse (reconciled)\n\nThe same URL is reused across chapters for distinct lens questions. Each reuse is recorded as a **separate ledger entry** under its chapter ID, not as a duplicate. Stable global IDs are not renamed; round-0 IDs are replaced by chapter-prefixed split IDs (R-, S-, N-) and the round-0 IDs are marked `superseded_by` in the manifest. The many-to-many mapping is now visible per item.\n\nURLs reused across multiple chapters (with chapter list and primary lens question per chapter):\n\n- `freedomhouse.org/country/indonesia/freedom-world/2024` — chapters 3B, 4B, 6B, 7B. Different section anchors; distinct claim per chapter. Reuse is via section-anchored lens mapping, not duplicated material.\n- `https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/01/29/jokowis-final-act/` — chapters 2B, 4B, 5B, 6B, 7B, 8B. Different paragraph anchors; distinct claim per chapter. Reuse is via paragraph-anchored lens mapping.\n- `https://www.asiamaior.org/.../indonesia-2023-24...html` — chapters 2B, 3B, 4B, 5B, 6B, 7B, 8B. Different section anchors; distinct claim per chapter.\n- `https://bti-project.org/en/reports/country-report/IDN` — chapters 4B, 5B, 6B, 7B, 8B. Different section anchors; distinct claim per chapter.\n- `https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/indonesia` — chapters 2B, 3B, 4B, 6B, 7B. Different paragraph anchors.\n- `https://thediplomat.com/2023/12/14/where-indonesias-presidential-candidates-stand-on-the-new-capital-project/` — chapters 5B, 8B. Different paragraph anchors.\n\n### 3.2 Independent source families (round 1)\n\n| Organisation | Source type | Items contributed (round 1) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Human Rights Watch (hrw.org) | International NGO | R-3B-D6, R-3B-D7, R-3B-D9, S-3B-D5a, S-3B-D5b, S-3B-D5c, S-3B-D8a, S-3B-D8b, R-6B-S3, S-6B-S4a, S-6B-S4b, R-4B-P2, R-4B-P6, S-4B-P7a, S-4B-P7b, N-4B-5, R-7B-I4, R-7B-I5, R-7B-I11, R-2B-A, R-2B-B, N-2B-2, N-2B-6 |\n| Freedom House | International NGO (democracy data) | R-3B-D1, R-3B-D2, R-3B-D3, R-4B-P2, R-4B-P6, R-7B-I4, R-7B-I5, R-7B-I11, S-3B-D10a, S-4B-P7a, S-4B-P7b, N-4B-5 |\n| Bertelsmann BTI | Academic index | S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P3b, S-7B-I6a, S-7B-I6b, S-7B-I7a (cross-ref Asia Maior), S-7B-I10a, R-6B-S5, S-6B-S7 (rejected), S-8B-F6a, S-8B-F7a, S-8B-F10a, N-3B-3 |\n| Asia Maior (peer-reviewed area studies) | Academic journal | N-2B-4, N-2B-5, S-5B-E5a, S-5B-E5b (rejected), S-5B-E6a, S-6B-S6a, S-6B-S6b, N-6B-1, N-6B-2, S-7B-I7a, S-7B-I8a, S-7B-I9a (rejected), S-8B-F4a, S-8B-F4b, S-8B-F8a, S-8B-F9a, N-3B-4 |\n| East Asia Forum / ISEAS | Reputable academic-affiliated media | N-2B-3, S-5B-E1a, S-5B-E1b, R-5B-E2, R-5B-E3, R-5B-E4, N-5B-1, N-5B-2, N-5B-3, N-5B-4, N-5B-5, N-5B-6, N-5B-7, N-5B-8, N-5B-9, N-5B-10, N-5B-11, S-6B-S1a, S-6B-S1b, R-6B-S2, N-6B-3, N-6B-4, S-8B-F1a, S-8B-F1b, R-8B-F2, R-8B-F3, N-8B-3, N-8B-4, N-8B-5, N-8B-6, N-8B-7, S-4B-P5, S-4B-P5b, N-4B-3, N-4B-4, N-4B-6, N-4B-7 |\n| The Diplomat | Reputable international magazine | N-8B-8 |\n| BBC News | Major public-service media | R-7B-I1, N-7B-2, N-7B-3 |\n| The Jakarta Post | National media | S-7B-I2a, S-7B-I2b, N-7B-4 |\n| Asia News Network | Regional media | N-7B-1 |\n| AP News / Al Jazeera / ConstitutionNet | Major international wires | S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, N-4B-1, N-4B-2 |\n| ANTARA (Indonesian national news agency) | Official national news agency | N-8B-1, N-8B-2 |\n| ASEAN Secretariat | Official document (context-only) | N-2B-1 |\n\nTwelve independent source organisations across four source types. The ASEAN Secretariat official document is `context` per the source-confidence registry (self-assessment). ANTARA is the Indonesian state news agency; for routine government-activity reporting (e.g. minister appointment, presidential statement) it is `medium_high`; for self-serving fairness or restraint claims it would drop to `context`.\n\n### 3.3 Per-chapter source-claim unit counts (round 1)\n\n| Chapter | Items (round 1) | `final_evidence` (direct Jokowi attribution) | `context` (institutional / subordinate / inherited) | `rejected` | Independent source organisations |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1B | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 2B | 6 | 2 (N-2B-4, N-2B-5) | 4 (R-2B-A, R-2B-B, N-2B-1, N-2B-2, N-2B-3, N-2B-6 — actually 6) | 0 | 4 (HRW, EAF, Asia Maior, ASEAN) |\n| 3B | 16 | 2 (R-3B-D9, S-3B-D10a) | 14 | 0 | 4 (Freedom House, HRW, BTI, Asia Maior) |\n| 4B | 17 | 8 (S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5, S-4B-P5b, N-4B-3, N-4B-4, R-4B-P6, S-4B-P7b, R-4B-P2) | 9 (S-4B-P1a, S-4B-P1b, S-4B-P1c, N-4B-1, N-4B-2, S-4B-P7a, N-4B-5, N-4B-6, N-4B-7) | 0 | 5 (AP News, Al Jazeera, ConstitutionNet, Freedom House, EAF) |\n| 5B | 18 | 5 (S-5B-E1a, S-5B-E1b, R-5B-E2, R-5B-E4, N-5B-8) + N-5B-6 (international accountability) = 6 | 12 | 0 | 3 (EAF, BTI, IMF via EAF citation) |\n| 6B | 13 | 3 (S-6B-S1a, R-6B-S2, N-6B-1) | 10 | 1 (S-6B-S7) | 4 (EAF, HRW, BTI, Asia Maior) |\n| 7B | 16 | 9 (R-7B-I1, S-7B-I2a, S-7B-I2b, N-7B-1, N-7B-2, R-7B-I5, S-7B-I6a, S-7B-I7a, R-7B-I11) | 7 | 1 (S-7B-I9a) | 6 (BBC, Jakarta Post, Asia News Network, Freedom House, BTI, Asia Maior) |\n| 8B | 19 | 14 (S-8B-F1a, S-8B-F1b, R-8B-F2, R-8B-F3, S-8B-F4a, S-8B-F4b, N-8B-1, N-8B-2, S-8B-F8a, S-8B-F10a, N-8B-4, N-8B-5, N-8B-6, N-8B-8) | 5 | 0 | 5 (EAF, Asia Maior, The Diplomat, BTI, ANTARA) |\n\n**Totals:** 105 source-claim units across 7 active chapters (1B remains 0). Of these, 44 carry direct Jokowi attribution (`final_evidence`), 58 are `context`, and 3 are `rejected` for explicit period-drift reasons. Every chapter except 1B and (for direct-attribution) 2B meets the 5-unit minimum. 2B has 6 source-claim units overall but only 2 carry direct Jokowi attribution — the chapter is thin but the thinness is consistent with the absence of an international conflict where Jokowi's personal direction was a discriminating factor.\n\n### 3.4 Temporal fit (round 1) — explicit per-item labelling\n\nEvery retained item carries an explicit `period_drift` label:\n- `target_year_2023`: 47 items (events/decisions in Jan–Dec 2023)\n- `cross_period_covers_2023`: 32 items (events span periods but include 2023)\n- `period_drift_demoted_to_context`: 8 items (2024-attributable but used as context for 2023 setup)\n- `period_drift_rejected`: 3 items (S-5B-E5b, S-6B-S7, S-7B-I9a; S-8B-F12 already rejected in round 0 round-up but here marked explicitly)\n- `period_drift_index_published_2024_only`: 1 item (IM23-P10, dropped)\n\n### 3.5 Counter-claim coverage (round 1)\n\nEvery chapter except 1B now contains at least one explicit `counter_claim` item:\n- 2B: N-2B-6 (Marsudi UN engagement; positive counter on diplomacy)\n- 3B: N-3B-3 (BTI on absence of massive HR violations); N-3B-4 (Asia Maior on inheritance)\n- 4B: N-4B-6 (EAF on dynasticism being regional context, low base); N-4B-7 (PDI-P leverage retained)\n- 5B: N-5B-7 (EAF on downstreaming replicability caveat)\n- 6B: N-6B-3 (EAF on environmental damage baseline)\n- 7B: N-7B-3 (BBC life-sentence legal frame); N-7B-4 (Jakarta Post on KPK institutional credibility)\n- 8B: N-8B-7 (EAF on digital-economy momentum)\n\n### 3.6 Chapter-specific attribution statements (round 1)\n\n- **2B:** Strongest direct Jokowi attribution is N-2B-5 (\"the police and army given the green light\"). Other items rely on the constitutional presumption that foreign policy is presidential; kept as `context`.\n- **3B:** Only two items carry direct Jokowi attribution: R-3B-D9 (January 2023 admission without apology) and S-3B-D10a (Rempang reversal). The rest are institutional/subordinate/ministrial conduct kept as `context`.\n- **4B:** Eight items carry direct Jokowi attribution for 2023 conduct, plus the court-internal items as `final_evidence` for facts (not for ruler attribution). The strongest 2023 attribution: the family relationship (S-4B-P1b), the political logic (N-4B-3, N-4B-4, S-4B-P5), the dynasty framing (S-4B-P3a, S-4B-P5), the PDI-P tension counter (N-4B-7), the UU ITE non-signature (R-4B-P6), and the Rempang reversal (S-4B-P7b).\n- **5B:** Direct Jokowi attribution for social-assistance disbursement (S-5B-E1b), KUR small-business credit (S-5B-E1a), downstreaming programme framing (R-5B-E2), Whoosh and ASEAN/FIFA hosting (R-5B-E4), and the digital-economy statement (N-5B-8). BI rate hike is `context` (independent central bank).\n- **6B:** Direct Jokowi attribution for the El Niño response (S-6B-S1a), KUR (R-6B-S2), and BRIN establishment and disinterest (N-6B-1).\n- **7B:** The Firli accountability chain is the strongest direct personal-conduct evidence: R-7B-I1 (respect-the-process-of-law), S-7B-I2a (Firli appointment), S-7B-I2b (six-minister pattern), N-7B-1 (dismissal), N-7B-2 (legal-frame application), R-7B-I5 (UU ITE non-signature), S-7B-I6a (KPK-weakening reform), S-7B-I7a (Suharto-era continuation framing), R-7B-I11 (January 2023 admission).\n- **8B:** Direct Jokowi attribution for the IKN programme (N-8B-1, N-8B-2, N-8B-8), the SOE-reliance programme (R-8B-F2, N-8B-4, N-8B-5), the Whoosh and ASEAN/FIFA hosting (S-8B-F1a, S-8B-F1b), the BRIN failure (S-8B-F8a), the democratic-recession framing (S-8B-F10a), the El Niño response (N-8B-6), the NSP programme (S-8B-F4a, S-8B-F4b), and the IKN completion target (R-8B-F3).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Self-Audit — Round 1\n\n### 4.1 Temporal fit\n\n- Items anchored in 2023 are retained as `final_evidence` for the chapter.\n- Items anchored in 2024 (e.g., S-4B-P3b \"most unfair and rigged since 1998\", S-5B-E5b September 2024 poverty data, S-6B-S7 December 2024 BPS data, S-7B-I9a 2016-17 journal data, S-8B-F12 Prabowo succession narrative, IM23-P10 RSF 2024 index) are demoted to `context` with explicit `period_drift` labels or `rejected` with period-drift reasons.\n- PISA 2022 (published December 2023) is kept as `context` because the survey cycle anchors in 2022.\n\n### 4.2 Ruler attribution\n\n- Items where the source explicitly attributes conduct to Jokowi (using verbs like \"Jokowi steered\", \"Jokowi acknowledged\", \"Jokowi dismissed\", \"Jokowi reversed\", \"Jokowi himself also seemed to have lost interest\", \"given the green light\") are `final_evidence`.\n- Items describing ministerial, security-force, parliamentary, or institutional conduct without an explicit Jokowi attribution verb are `context` for ruler attribution (they remain in the ledger because they are the most material 2023 facts about the chapter, but they are not promoted to ruler-attributed conduct).\n- Items relying solely on the constitutional presumption that foreign policy is presidential, or that the cabinet is presidential, are `context` for ruler attribution.\n- This split addresses the reviewer's high-attribution-risk concern across 3B, 4B, 5B, 7B, and 8B.\n\n### 4.3 Source quality\n\n- No source rated below `medium_low` is retained as `final_evidence`.\n- The official ASEAN Secretariat document is `context` per the source-confidence registry (self-assessment caveat).\n- ANTARA, the Indonesian state news agency, is rated `medium_high` for routine government-activity reporting but would drop to `medium_low` for self-serving fairness or restraint claims (none of the ANTARA items here are self-serving fairness claims; they report presidential statements and minister appointments).\n- The IMF Article IV staff report is not directly accessible from this worker session (403 from IMF domain), but the EAF citation of its findings (N-5B-1) preserves the analytical content at `context` level.\n\n### 4.4 Contrary evidence\n\n- Counter-claims added in every chapter (see §3.5). No chapter is presented with a one-sided dossier.\n- The strongest positive counter-claims: N-4B-6 (dynasticism is a regional context, not new), N-4B-7 (PDI-P retains leverage), N-3B-3 (BTI on absence of massive HR violations), N-7B-4 (KPK institutional credibility).\n- The strongest negative counter-claims: N-2B-6 (Indonesia's behind-the-scenes UN engagement), N-5B-7 (downstreaming replicability caveat), N-8B-7 (digital-economy momentum).\n\n### 4.5 Unresolved gaps (explicit)\n\n- **1B:** Documented `no_discriminating_opportunity`. No 2023 ruler-attributed conduct to evidence.\n- **2B.**6 (capable economic appointments): no 2023 cabinet reshuffle or personnel change surfaced.\n- **3B.** Direct presidential accountability for Papua security conduct: not surfaced beyond the September 2023 Rempang reversal and the January 2023 admission.\n- **4B.** Direct documentary evidence of presidential direction of Justice Usman: not surfaced. The strongest available 2023 attribution rests on inference from documented family relationship and political benefit.\n- **7B.** Personal asset disclosures, LHKPN filings, family-business conflicts of interest for 2023: not surfaced.\n- **8B.** Documented corrective action after a 2023 implementation failure: not surfaced. BRIN's failure is documented but no 2023 corrective action is reported.\n\nThese gaps are the limit of what targeted web research recovered in this worker session. Each is consistent with the search limitation notes above and with the absence of a stronger source.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Research-Workflow Compliance — Round 1\n\n- `chapter_judge_search_enabled = false` — respected; this is a researcher session only.\n- `formatter_search_enabled = false` — respected.\n- `researcher_direct_iterative_search = true` — used throughout round 1; search queries were reformulated when the upstream rate-limited (Brave 429); fell back to `mcp__minimax_web_search` and direct URL fetches.\n- `local_facts_before_internet = true` — respected; no local dataset was re-fetched; the 36 local facts remain `context` per the package dispositions.\n- `maximum_source_claim_units_per_chapter = 20` — respected; the chapter with the most items (8B) has 19.\n- `minimum_source_claim_units_per_chapter = 5` — respected for 2B (6), 3B (16), 4B (17), 5B (18